{"@context":["https://litlfred.github.io/folio-assistant/ns/content/v1.jsonld",{"@base":"https://litlfred.github.io/folio/"}],"@id":"papers/quantum-observable-universe/blocks/rem-descartes-elliptic-curve-interpretation","@type":["folio-assistant-core:Remark","doco:Section"],"label":"rem:descartes-elliptic-curve-interpretation","kind":"remark","title":"Sub-classical regime and the $q$-dependence of color-fibration stability","uses":["papers/quantum-observable-universe/blocks/prop-descartes-elliptic-curve"],"interprets":"papers/quantum-observable-universe/blocks/prop-descartes-elliptic-curve","text":"descartes-elliptic-curve-interpretation.md","provenance":"authored","html":"<a id=\"rem:descartes-elliptic-curve-interpretation\"></a><p>This identifies the <strong>sub-classical regime</strong> <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">q &#x3C; 1</code>. Here, the substrate parameter reverses its role; the quantum deformation acts in the opposite direction. The golden ratio threshold <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">q = 1/\\varphi^2</code> marks the boundary beyond which the Descartes universe cannot support stable color structure.</p>\n<p><strong><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">q</code>-Dependence of the stability structure.</strong>\nAs <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">q</code> varies from <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">1</code> to <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">\\infty</code>:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Regime</th>\n<th><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">q</code></th>\n<th><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">j(\\mathcal{E}_q)</code></th>\n<th>Physics</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Classical</td>\n<td><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">q = 1</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">128</code></td>\n<td>No crossing asymmetry; <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">E_1</code> is smooth</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Sub-golden</td>\n<td><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">q &#x3C; 1/\\varphi^2</code></td>\n<td>undefined</td>\n<td><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">\\mathcal{E}_q</code> singular; no stable color fibers</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Golden threshold</td>\n<td><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">q = 1/\\varphi^2</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">\\to \\infty</code></td>\n<td>Cusp; color degeneration</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Physical</td>\n<td>the substrate <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">q</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">129.9</code></td>\n<td>Our universe; <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">\\mathcal{E}_q</code> smooth with <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">\\Delta &#x3C; 0</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Deep quantum</td>\n<td><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">q \\to \\infty</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">\\to 0</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">j \\to 0</code>; curve approaches CM curve</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n","source":"folio/quantum-observable-universe/brings-surface/descartes-elliptic-curve-interpretation.md","heading":"Remark","number":"7.8.22"}
